1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,358
Aarav adds seven and five on paper.

2
00:00:02,358 --> 00:00:04,139
He writes two, and carries one.

3
00:00:04,439 --> 00:00:06,780
His calculator gets twelve at once.

4
00:00:06,780 --> 00:00:09,072
Inside, it only has logic gates.

5
00:00:09,372 --> 00:00:11,123
How can gates add numbers?

6
00:00:11,123 --> 00:00:13,121
They use the same carry trick.

7
00:00:13,621 --> 00:00:15,120
Calculators count in binary.

8
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:16,303
Only zeros and ones.

9
00:00:16,653 --> 00:00:18,546
Zero plus zero is zero.

10
00:00:18,546 --> 00:00:20,328
Zero plus one is one.

11
00:00:20,678 --> 00:00:22,402
But one plus one is two.

12
00:00:22,402 --> 00:00:24,802
In binary, two is written one zero.

13
00:00:25,152 --> 00:00:27,302
So we write zero, and carry one.

14
00:00:27,302 --> 00:00:28,980
Just like seven plus five.

15
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:31,786
So every column needs two answers.

16
00:00:31,786 --> 00:00:33,764
A sum bit, and a carry bit.

17
00:00:34,414 --> 00:00:37,439
A circuit that adds two
bits is called a half adder.

18
00:00:37,789 --> 00:00:39,333
It has two inputs, A and B.

19
00:00:39,333 --> 00:00:40,978
And two outputs, sum and carry.

20
00:00:41,328 --> 00:00:43,940
Let's fill its truth
table on the breadboard.

21
00:00:44,290 --> 00:00:45,569
Zero plus zero.

22
00:00:45,569 --> 00:00:46,469
Sum zero.

23
00:00:46,457 --> 00:00:47,454
Carry zero.

24
00:00:47,804 --> 00:00:50,193
Zero plus one, or one plus zero.

25
00:00:50,193 --> 00:00:51,093
Sum one.

26
00:00:50,970 --> 00:00:51,897
Carry zero.

27
00:00:52,247 --> 00:00:53,416
One plus one.

28
00:00:53,416 --> 00:00:54,316
Sum zero.

29
00:00:54,304 --> 00:00:55,246
Carry one.

30
00:00:55,596 --> 00:00:57,082
Look at the sum column.

31
00:00:57,082 --> 00:00:59,382
It is one only when the inputs differ.

32
00:01:00,032 --> 00:01:02,088
That is an XOR gate.

33
00:01:02,088 --> 00:01:03,970
S equals AXORB.

34
00:01:04,620 --> 00:01:07,219
The carry is one only when both are one.

35
00:01:07,219 --> 00:01:08,179
That is AND.

36
00:01:08,179 --> 00:01:09,241
C equals AB.

37
00:01:09,891 --> 00:01:13,605
So a half adder is just one
XOR gate and one AND gate.

38
00:01:14,255 --> 00:01:16,464
But why is it called a half adder?

39
00:01:16,464 --> 00:01:18,346
It has no input for a carry.

40
00:01:18,696 --> 00:01:22,832
In bigger sums, each column can get
a carry from the column on its right.

41
00:01:23,182 --> 00:01:24,842
So we must add three bits.

42
00:01:24,842 --> 00:01:26,546
A, B, and the carry in.

43
00:01:26,896 --> 00:01:28,566
That circuit is the full adder.

44
00:01:28,566 --> 00:01:30,026
Three inputs, two outputs.

45
00:01:30,376 --> 00:01:33,150
The sum is one when an odd
number of inputs are one.

46
00:01:33,800 --> 00:01:37,242
So S equals AXORBXOR carry in.

47
00:01:37,892 --> 00:01:40,503
The carry out is one when
two or more inputs are one.

48
00:01:41,153 --> 00:01:45,267
C out equals AB, plus
carry in times AXORB.

49
00:01:45,917 --> 00:01:47,371
Here is the neat part.

50
00:01:47,371 --> 00:01:49,646
We can build it from two half adders.

51
00:01:49,996 --> 00:01:52,288
The first half adder adds A and B.

52
00:01:52,638 --> 00:01:55,317
The second adds that sum to the carry in.

53
00:01:55,317 --> 00:01:57,016
That gives the final sum.

54
00:01:57,366 --> 00:02:01,061
If either half adder makes a
carry, an OR gate passes it on.

55
00:02:01,711 --> 00:02:05,265
So two half adders and one
OR gate make one full adder.

56
00:02:05,915 --> 00:02:09,724
Chain four full adders, and you
can add two four bit numbers.

57
00:02:10,374 --> 00:02:11,825
Sum is XOR.

58
00:02:11,825 --> 00:02:13,130
Carry is AND.

59
00:02:13,930 --> 00:02:14,989
Worked example.

60
00:02:14,989 --> 00:02:17,673
Add one, one and one with a full adder.

61
00:02:18,023 --> 00:02:21,346
A is one, B is one, and
the carry in is one.

62
00:02:22,046 --> 00:02:23,329
First half adder.

63
00:02:23,329 --> 00:02:26,115
One plus one gives sum zero, carry one.

64
00:02:26,815 --> 00:02:28,167
Second half adder.

65
00:02:28,167 --> 00:02:31,044
Zero plus one gives sum one, carry zero.

66
00:02:31,744 --> 00:02:32,661
The OR gate.

67
00:02:32,661 --> 00:02:35,011
One or zero gives a carry out of one.

68
00:02:35,711 --> 00:02:38,880
So the sum is one, and
the carry out is one.

69
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:40,302
That reads one one.

70
00:02:41,002 --> 00:02:42,849
One one in binary is three.

71
00:02:42,849 --> 00:02:45,187
And one plus one plus one is three.

72
00:02:45,837 --> 00:02:47,300
Now, the exam trap.

73
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:50,869
Many students use an OR gate
for the sum of a half adder.

74
00:02:51,219 --> 00:02:52,505
Check one plus one.

75
00:02:52,505 --> 00:02:54,309
OR would give a sum of one.

76
00:02:54,659 --> 00:02:58,071
But the right sum is
zero, with a carry of one.

77
00:02:58,071 --> 00:02:59,706
The sum needs XOR.

78
00:03:00,356 --> 00:03:02,038
Where is this asked?

79
00:03:02,038 --> 00:03:06,305
In PUC board papers, and
in ISC Computer Science.

80
00:03:06,655 --> 00:03:10,653
Expect truth tables, the sum and
carry formulas, and a full adder from

81
00:03:10,653 --> 00:03:11,739
two half adders.

82
00:03:12,389 --> 00:03:13,289
Try this.

83
00:03:13,170 --> 00:03:15,333
Add binary one one and zero one.

84
00:03:15,333 --> 00:03:17,543
A half adder adds the right bits.

85
00:03:17,543 --> 00:03:19,706
A full adder adds the left bits.

86
00:03:19,706 --> 00:03:21,078
What is the answer?

87
00:03:26,478 --> 00:03:27,378
B.

88
00:03:26,899 --> 00:03:28,061
One zero zero.

89
00:03:28,411 --> 00:03:29,311
Right bits.

90
00:03:29,294 --> 00:03:30,332
One plus one.

91
00:03:30,332 --> 00:03:31,859
Sum zero, carry one.

92
00:03:32,209 --> 00:03:33,109
Left bits.

93
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:35,846
One plus zero, plus the carry of one.

94
00:03:36,196 --> 00:03:38,711
That gives sum zero,
and a carry out of one.

95
00:03:39,061 --> 00:03:41,320
So the answer is one zero zero.

96
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:43,050
Three plus one is four.

97
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:45,247
Screenshot this card.

98
00:03:48,447 --> 00:03:49,858
Next, flip-flops.

99
00:03:49,858 --> 00:03:51,481
SR, JK and D.

100
00:03:51,481 --> 00:03:53,359
Gates that can remember.
